 (Δ +1.9 dB, 5.1 mm in 72 h — coincident with precipitation); 2026-04-05 (Δ -1.9 dB, 0.6 mm in 72 h); 2026-10-21 (Δ +1.8 dB, 19.0 mm in 72 h — coincident with precipitation); 2026-11-02 (Δ -1.9 dB, 9.3 mm in 72 h — coincident with precipitation). Backscatter increases coincident with precipitation are consistent with a possible increase in surface moisture, without proving the cause. The changes of 2026-01-17, 2026-04-05, 2026-11-02 reverse the previous one: possible return to the previous level (e.g. drying), i.e. a transient episode rather than a persistent change. Between the last two passes, 0.0 % of the pixels (filtered to 50 m) changed by more than 3 dB.","license":"CC BY 4.0 (Sentinel-1 RTC, Planetary Computer)","credit":"Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data 2025–2026","source":"https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/sentinel-1-rtc","notes":["Orbit used: relative 89 (ascending), VV polarization; 61 acquisitions of that orbit in the period, 25 selected. Other orbits present: 23 descending (58).","Robust site σ_Δ = 0.13 dB; flagged scenes: 6 of 24 pairs.","Fraction of pixels with |Δ| > 3 dB in the last unfiltered pair: 0.011 (speckle-dominated; the published value uses the 5×5 filter).","A backscatter change can be due to soil moisture (rain/dew), sand migration or remobilization, snow/ice or frost, ephemeral vegetation or residual geometry/calibration differences; this product does not attribute causes.","Reanalysis precipitation is a cell-scale estimate (tens of km) and may not reflect local rain."]}
